<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Börte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1161–c. 1230</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Börte was the first and principal wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>, later Genghis Khan, and mother of his four main sons, Jochi, Chagatai, Ögedei and Tolui. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a>, she was abducted by the Merkits soon after her marriage and rescued in a campaign led by Temüjin with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ong-khan">Toghrul</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamukha">Jamukha</a>. As senior empress she held great authority.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Few facts are known about Börte's early life, and what is known comes largely from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a>, the oldest surviving literary work in the Mongolian language. She was born around 1161 into the Olkhonud of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khongirad">Khongirad</a>, a people friendly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a>, the tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>. Her parents were Dei-Sechen and Chotan.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a> was nine, his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yesugei">Yesügei</a> met Dei-Sechen on a journey to find a bride. Dei-Sechen described a dream that he took as a good omen, and Yesügei accepted Börte, then ten, as Temüjin's future wife, with Temüjin to stay with her family. On the way home Yesügei was poisoned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and died, and Temüjin returned to his family. In 1178, after seven years apart, he went back to Börte and married her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Soon after the marriage the Three Merkit attacked the camp on the Burgi Escarpment. Temüjin's family escaped on horses, but Börte was left behind and was captured. The raid was said to be revenge for the earlier abduction of Temüjin's mother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoelun">Hö'elün</a>, from the Merkit. Temüjin sought help from Ong Qan, who had received Börte's dowry, a black sable coat, and after about eight months they attacked the Merkit and recovered her. The Secret History tells how she recognised his voice and ran to him.</p><p>Börte brought independent wealth from her dowry and shared the management of the camp's people, animals and economy with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoelun">Hö'elün</a>. Her nine children, four sons and five daughters, were central to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>'s rule: the daughters married allies, and the sons took part in his conquests. She advised him to break with the military leader Jamuqa, and she was named Grand Empress after he became Genghis Khan. While he campaigned she stayed behind and helped his brother Temüge govern the homeland, holding her own court and much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kherlen-river">Kherlen River</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Doubts about the true father of her eldest son Jochi, who was born after her captivity, led to quarrels between Jochi and his brother Chagatai. Genghis Khan denied them and claimed Jochi as his son, but he chose neither of them as his successor. Ögedei was selected instead, and Jochi's descendants founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>.</p><p>Börte died in 1230, after her husband. She was revered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> as a mother figure, a standing helped by the orphans she adopted and raised as her own, including Qutqu Noyan and Buda Noyan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Börte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the Secret History of the Mongols, Temüjin's father Yesügei had stolen his wife Hö'elün from a Merkit, and in revenge the Merkits abducted Temüjin's bride <b>Börte</b>, who was rescued with help from Toghrul of the Keraite Khanate and Jamukha.</blockquote><a href="/state/merkit-confederation">Merkit confederation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Khentii Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Died in Avarga, in what is now <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48,110.5&amp;year=1205" aria-label="Khentii Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7122 483 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Börte"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7572 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7572 753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48,110.5&amp;year=1205">What was here in 1205? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
